.TH "NOTIFICATION.ICON" "3JS" "October 11, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Notification.icon \- Notification: icon property
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Secure context:\f[R] This feature is available only in secure
contexts (HTTPS).
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]icon\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]Notification\f[R] interface returns a string containing the URL of
a small image that reinforces the notification \(em for example, an app
logo or a photo of the message sender.
It is displayed alongside the notification title and body.
.PP
See Notifications with different images for a comparison of the three
image options.
.SH VALUE
A string.
.SH EXAMPLES
In our \c
.UR https://github.com/mdn/dom-examples/tree/main/to-do-notifications
To\-do list app
.UE \c
\ (\c
.UR https://mdn.github.io/dom-examples/to-do-notifications/
view the app running live
.UE \c
), we use the \f[CR]Notification()\f[R] constructor to fire a
notification, passing it arguments to specify the body, icon and title
we want.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] notification = \f[B]new\f[R] Notification(\(dqTo do list\(dq, {
  body: text,
  icon: img,
});
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
Using the Notifications API
